---
// Native details/summary keeps the FAQ usable without client JavaScript.
---

<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <h2 id="faq-title">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="questions">
    <details name="faq">
      <summary>What is a doctor?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        A doctor is a JavaScript file that scans your repo for specific kinds of
        mistakes. It could scan for patterns you've seen from code reviews,
        library specific anti-patterns, stylistic coding practices, and do
        cross-file linting. Normally, you'd keep this kind of guidance in a
        skill, but a doctor searches for those patterns deterministically,
        saving you and your team time and money.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>How is this different from ESLint or Oxlint?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a">
        <p><strong>DO NOT STOP USING LINT TOOLS!</strong> Use ESLint or Oxlint whenever an existing rule handles the problem well.</p>
        <p>Create a doctor when:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The rule is specific to your repository or team.</li>
          <li>The same feedback keeps appearing in code reviews.</li>
          <li>Detecting the issue requires looking across files.</li>
          <li>The guidance lives in a skill or document, but agents still miss it.</li>
          <li>You want to check a known pattern without paying for another LLM review.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Doctors sit between traditional linting and open-ended AI review: deterministic and inexpensive to run like a linter, but tailored to the project-specific knowledge you would otherwise ask an agent to remember.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>Does it replace AI or human code review?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        No. Doctors handle stable, repeatable observations without model
        inference. Keep AI and human reviewers for product intent, architecture,
        security, unfamiliar situations, and anything that still requires judgment.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>What happens when a finding is intentional?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        Mark it accepted, or mark the check not applicable, and record why. The
        finding leaves the active list but remains visible in the reviewed view.
        Changed evidence or changed doctor meaning brings it back for reassessment.
        A genuine detector false positive should become a fixture and improve the doctor.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>Does scanning send my source code anywhere?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        No. Scans run locally, without an Any Doctor account, API key, or hosted
        scoring service. Doctor processes have no network, subprocess, or
        project-write authority. If an external agent helps author a doctor, that
        provider's data policy applies to the authoring conversation.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>Does Any Doctor support every language?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        Not yet. Any Doctor currently supports JavaScript and TypeScript. We
        plan to explore support for other languages in the future.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>Can it run in CI?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        It is designed for CI with JSON output, exit codes, and base-branch
        comparison so a change can be judged by newly introduced findings. Pin
        the package version for reproducibility while Any Doctor is pre-1.0.
      </p></div>
    </details>

    <details name="faq">
      <summary>Why “doctor”?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
      <div class="a"><p>
        This project is heavily inspired by React Doctor. Imagine having that
        “doctor” experience, but for checks tailored specifically to your org.
        That's the goal of this project.
      </p></div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .faq { margin-top: 5.5rem; }
  .faq > h2 { margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: var(--color-foreground); font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: -0.025em; font-weight: 700; }
  details { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); interpolate-size: allow-keywords; }
  details:first-of-type { border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  details::details-content { height: 0; overflow: hidden; opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-0.3rem); transition: height 260ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), opacity 180ms ease, transform 240ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), content-visibility 260ms allow-discrete; }
  details[open]::details-content { height: auto; opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  @starting-style {
    details[open]::details-content { height: 0; opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-0.3rem); }
  }
  summary { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; padding: 1.05rem 0.25rem; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; font-weight: 650; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-foreground); }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary); outline-offset: 4px; border-radius: 4px; }
  .chev { width: 1rem; height: 1rem; flex-shrink: 0; color: var(--color-muted-foreground); transition: transform 180ms ease; }
  details[open] .chev { transform: rotate(90deg); }
  details[open] summary { color: var(--color-primary); }
  .a { padding: 0 2rem 1.2rem 0.25rem; }
  .a p { max-width: 65ch; margin: 0; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-foreground) 78%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); font-size: 0.94rem; line-height: 1.75; }
  .a p + p, .a ul + p { margin-top: 0.85rem; }
  .a strong { color: var(--color-foreground); font-weight: 750; }
  .a ul { max-width: 65ch; margin: 0.45rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; list-style: disc; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-foreground) 78%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); font-size: 0.94rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .a li::marker { color: var(--color-primary); }
  .a li + li { margin-top: 0.25rem; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .faq { margin-top: 4.5rem; } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    details::details-content, .chev { transition: none; }
  }
</style>
